/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<CLIFF_GENERATION>
  min_number_of_cliffs 20
  max_number_of_cliffs 20
  min_length_of_cliff 3
  max_length_of_cliff 3
  cliff_curliness 10
  min_distance_cliffs 4

<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

  create_object FORAGE
  {
    number_of_objects 25
    set_gaia_object_only
  }

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

/*
RMSTEST_45b - arm 2 of 3, and the arm carrying the full question.
Feeds: preview-design Sec.6.3, cliffs.ts, Sec.15 item 17(b)

THIS ARM: minimum 3, maximum 3. Exactly at the documented floor. This is the
positive control - if this arm produces no cliffs either, the map is wrong and
all three arms are void.

QUESTION
The guide states that a minimum cliff length below 3 produces no cliffs at all.
cliffs.ts implements that as zero cliffs plus a note, never as a clamp, because
the spec instructs it. But six official maps write a minimum of 1 or 2 in a live
unconditional block, one of them asking for five to ten cliffs at length two to
four. Under the shipped rule every one of those blocks is dead.

The rule is the working model and there is a reason to keep it - the map most
often cited is not remembered as having cliffs, which is the best observation
available and agrees with the guide. But it is unmeasured, and it silently
suppresses a whole section of six shipped maps. Verified, not assumed.

THE THREE ARMS
  45a  min 2 / max 2   both below the floor
  45b  min 3 / max 3   at the floor, the positive control
  45c  min 2 / max 4   straddling it

Everything else in the three files is identical: twenty cliffs requested, the
same curliness and spacing, the same flat grass map, the same control bushes.

WHAT EACH OUTCOME MEANS
  zero in 45a, normal in 45b, zero in 45c
      The documented rule is exactly right. A sub-3 minimum suppresses the
      section whatever the maximum says. cliffs.ts stands as written and the six
      shipped blocks really are dead. Strike the verify marker.

  45a about equal to 45b
      It is a CLAMP, not a suppression. The engine raises the minimum to 3 and
      carries on. cliffs.ts is wrong in the most consequential direction - it
      draws nothing where the engine draws cliffs - and six maps get their
      cliffs back.

  zero in 45a, PARTIAL count in 45c
      A per-draw yield. Each cliff rolls its own length in the declared band and
      only the rolls landing at 3 or above survive. 45c should then come out at
      roughly a third to a half of 45b. This is the outcome neither the guide
      nor the spec anticipates, and it is the one that needs a new rule rather
      than a corrected constant.

  anything nonzero in 45a
      The guide sentence is false as written and Sec.6.3 needs re-sourcing.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), any player count. TWO RUNS PER ARM, six generations
for the set. Two rather than one because the count is a distribution - the
zero-versus-nonzero question is binary but the clamp-versus-yield question is
not, and a single sample cannot separate a third from a half.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --clusters
  Count cliff units. Compare the three arms against each other, never against an
  absolute expectation - twenty declared cliffs is a request, not a promise.

RUN THIS SET SECOND, AFTER LOADING THE COMMUNITY MAP NAMED FOR ITS CLIFFS THAT
SETS A SUB-3 MINIMUM. If that map visibly has cliffs, arm 45a is already
answered and the clamp reading wins before a single generation is spent.
*/
